Ansible Roles:
 An Ansible Role breaks a monolithic playbook into specific subdirectories under a role name:
  • tasks/main.yml: Contains the main list of tasks executed by the role. 
  • handlers/main.yml: Contains handlers (like restarting services) triggered by tasks using notify.
  • defaults/main.yml: Low-priority default variables that users can easily override.
  • vars/main.yml: High-priority variables that are difficult to override; use for internal constants. 
  • files/: Stores static files copied to remote machines. 
  • templates/: Stores dynamic Jinja2 templates rendered before copying. 
  • meta/main.yml: Metadata including author information and role dependencies.

ansible configuration path preference can be changed in 4 types:
ansible --version to check ansible default path, i.e. /etc/ansible/ansible.cfg

1.ANSIBLE_CONFIG
2.PWD ansible.cfg
3.user home directory .ansible.cfg
4./etc/ansible.cfg

now in ansible.cfg file we already gave inventory file, hence we can close ansible-roles-repo and give ansible-playbook main.yaml
to execute tasks from demo/tasks folder.

Eg of Roles Execution:
first it executes roboshop.yaml, then checks for main in catalogue/meta/main.yaml file, and if it has any dependencies with common role 
which will be defined in meta/main.yaml file ,it will come and execute common/tasks/dependent file.yaml file 
and goes back to catalogue and then execute catalogue/tasks/main.yaml file which also 
has common code imported via ansible.builtin.import_role.

Same for other common code files also, eg: nodejs-setup, python-setup etc.

Handlers - to restart services when any configuration changes are made.

import role respects the tags, if you apply tags to import_role those will be applied automatically to all tasks inside that role,

include role does not respect tags, hence those will not be applied automatically to all tasks inside that role.

